# A browser fetches a module's imports before it can link them, and the
# pinned engine resolves every import as it compiles a module, through a
# loader that must answer at once, so an entry whose imports are not
# fetched yet fails to compile (upstream's own comment, "Could add a flag
# to avoid resolution if necessary", marks the spot). JS_EVAL_FLAG_NO_RESOLVE
# keeps a compiled-only module unresolved, and JS_GetModuleRequestCount /
# JS_GetModuleRequest name what it imports, so a host can fetch those and
# call JS_ResolveModule when every source is in hand. A module body that
# throws is run as an async function whose rejected promise the engine
# takes as the module's error: it is now made handled and the tracker told,
# or a host hears one module error twice (once as an unhandled rejection).
# And a failed resolve leaves no module it marked resolved, the members of
# a cycle included (each points back at the module that failed), so a host
# whose modules outlive the failure never links one through the NULL of an
# import never found, or through a module the failure freed.
